UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 7, Lesson 1: The design process', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"Design names the user and the problem before decoration, colour or stickers are chosen at all. A user is the person who will actually use the solution. A need is what is going wrong for that person. Jordan is a Year 7 student who does homework at a small desk. Pencils roll off the edge, drop to the floor and take time to find. The user is Jordan. The problem is lost rolling pencils, not a dull-looking desk. Stickers, a favourite colour or a fancy shape can be decided later, after the need is clear. If you decorate first, you may make something attractive that still lets pencils fall.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same technology/design idea to a new object or material, then compare two pieces of evidence for strength.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nJordan wants rainbow stickers on the organiser before writing anything else. State the user and the problem that must be written before any colour or sticker is chosen.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe user is Jordan and the problem is pencils rolling off the desk; rainbow stickers are not the need. Jordan is a Year 7 student working at a small desk. Pencils roll over the edge and are lost underneath. A sticker colour does not keep a pencil on the desk, so it is not part of the problem statement. Write the user and the lost-pencil problem first. Choose colour only after that need is clear.\n\nWATCH FOR\nStarting with rainbow stickers or a favourite colour before naming who loses the pencils. That mistake treats design as decorating. The pencils still roll away, so the need is untouched. Fix it by writing the user and the problem in two sentences, then leave colour until later.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nA classmate knocks an eraser off a crowded afternoon desk; name that user and problem with no colour. Write two sentences: who the user is, and what goes wrong. Do not choose stickers, colours or a shape. This is a different person and a different object from Jordan and the rolling pencils.\n\nWrite a short reflection naming one observation, criterion or step you corrected and why.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"Design and Technology","learning_intention":"Design names the user and the problem before decoration, colour or stickers are chosen at all. A user is the person who will actually use the solution. A need is what is going wrong for that person. Jordan is a Year 7 student who does homework at a small desk. Pencils roll off the edge, drop to the floor and take time to find. The user is Jordan. The problem is lost rolling pencils, not a dull-looking desk. Stickers, a favourite colour or a fancy shape can be decided later, after the need is clear. If you decorate first, you may make something attractive that still lets pencils fall.","lesson_number":1,"lesson_role":"Learn","materials":["Paper or typed answer space","Pencil","Optional: safe classroom tools/materials under teacher supervision","Device only when the lesson requires digital work"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper and pencil to complete the original task, then retain a photograph or short parent observation as evidence.","outcome_codes":["DIT5-EVL-01"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: A classmate knocks an eraser off a crowded afternoon desk; name that user and problem with no colour. Write two sentences: who the user is, and what goes wrong. Do not choose stickers, colours or a shape. This is a diffe Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Sample: original example using user on new material; names a specific word, source or choice and explains its effect.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Sample error: Starting with rainbow stickers or a favourite colour before naming who loses the pencils.. Correction: The user is Jordan and the problem is pencils rolling off the desk; rainbow stickers are not the need.. Why it helps the audience is stated.\n\nParent: Likely misconception: Starting with rainbow stickers or a favourite colour before naming who loses the pencils. That mistake treats design as decorating. The pencils still roll away, so the need is untouched. Fix it by writing the user and the problem in two sentences, then leave colour until later. Keep sample answers out of the child role.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 1: Produce a fresh example demonstrating user on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 2: Name one likely error with user, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.\n\nParent: Marking notes: Accept original work about user with a specific supporting detail. Reject copied model wording with no new material. / Accept a misconception, a correction, and a purpose-linked reason for user. Reject corrections that do not change the inaccurate idea..","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"This matches the taught method for user. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Copy the model objects and call that independent work","The user is Jordan and the problem is pencils rolling off the desk","Starting with rainbow stickers or a favourite colour before naming who loses the pencils","Ignore the observation or criteria and invent a more exciting result"],"question":"Jordan wants rainbow stickers on the organiser before writing anything else. State the user and the problem that must be written before any colour or sticker is chosen. Which response is most accurate?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"This is the misconception to avoid for user; the other choices align with the lesson method. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["The user is Jordan and the problem is pencils rolling off the desk","Careful use of need with clear evidence","Starting with rainbow stickers or a favourite colour before naming who loses the pencils","Design names the user and the problem before decoration, colour or stickers are chosen at all"],"question":"Which statement is a common mistake when working with user?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Understanding is shown by new evidence and a named need, not by copying the model. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Apply user to new material and name the need used","Repeat the worked example wording with no new evidence","Starting with rainbow stickers or a favourite colour before naming who loses the pencils","Name need only, without linking it to user"],"question":"Which response best applies user with new evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"The stronger choice uses design criteria, safe making steps or test evidence appropriately for this lesson on user. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Starting with rainbow stickers or a favourite colour before naming who loses the pencils","The user is Jordan and the problem is pencils rolling off the desk","Start making with no criteria or safety check","Call the first attempt finished without testing"],"question":"Which response best evaluates design criteria, safe making steps or test evidence when analysing user?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Strong answers name specific evidence and what it shows. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Start making with no criteria or safety check","Call the first attempt finished without testing","Starting with rainbow stickers or a favourite colour before naming who loses the pencils","Name a criterion, safety step or test result and say how it improves user"],"question":"Which evidence move best supports a claim about user?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"The stronger choice matches the taught idea (Design names the user and the problem before decoration, colour or) with concrete detail. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Starting with rainbow stickers or a favourite colour before naming who loses the pencils","Start making with no criteria or safety check","Call the first attempt finished without testing","Jordan is a Year 7 student whose pencils roll off a small homework desk and disappear underneath"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about user?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Accuracy means checking evidence and honouring limits or protocols. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Check criteria and safety, record a test result, and note one honest improvement","Invent missing details to make the answer sound complete","Ignore safety or cultural protocols to finish faster","Treat every claim as equally reliable without checking a source or cue"],"question":"Which action keeps design criteria, safe making steps or test evidence accurate for user?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Independence requires a new example that still uses the taught skill. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["A classmate knocks an eraser off a crowded afternoon desk","Retype the worked example with no new example","Starting with rainbow stickers or a favourite colour before naming who loses the pencils","Name need only, with no linked example"],"question":"Which independent response best applies user to new material?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence on new material demonstrates the skill; copying the model does not.","marking_guide":"Accept original work about user with a specific supporting detail. Reject copied model wording with no new material.","question":"Produce a fresh example demonstrating user on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.","sample_answer":"Sample: original example using user on new material; names a specific word, source or choice and explains its effect.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A useful correction changes the inaccurate idea or method and explains why.","marking_guide":"Accept a misconception, a correction, and a purpose-linked reason for user. Reject corrections that do not change the inaccurate idea.","question":"Name one likely error with user, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.","sample_answer":"Sample error: Starting with rainbow stickers or a favourite colour before naming who loses the pencils.. Correction: The user is Jordan and the problem is pencils rolling off the desk; rainbow stickers are not the need.. Why it helps the audience is stated.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent evidence and reflection, with reasons where requested.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s5-design-technology-y9-w01-l1","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Catalogue subject is Design and Technology. Proposed Year 7 alignment uses Design Innovation Technology 7–10 (2025) outcome DIT5-EVL-01, which the syllabus says may be adjusted for Stage 4 (DIT4-ADJ-01). The 2019 Design and Technology syllabus is in teach-out until 2029. Content-point mapping pending. Not NESA verified. Practical work is paper or cardboard only.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S4","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise user.","Read the mission before you start observing, designing or testing.","Keep the property, criterion or variable you are studying in view."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your evidence with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not match the method."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the observation, criterion or step that carries the idea.","Then write the reason without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore observations, criteria or fair tests.","Prefer accurate evidence over a guess that merely looks neat."],"submit":["Confirm that your evidence and reason are both present.","Proofread labels, measurements and sequence of steps.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked model.","Name one exact observation, criterion or test step before you decide.","Try the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the model and list the key observations or criteria.","Say what each observation or criterion shows about the idea.","A final label alone is weaker than recorded evidence."],"teach2":["Compare a weak claim with stronger evidence.","Change the claim or method, not only how neat the drawing looks.","Name the mistake you are repairing."],"teach3":["Choose a fresh object or context that still uses the same skill.","Write one reason in your own words.","Do not retype the worked model as your answer."],"write":["Follow every part of the independent instructions.","Include original evidence and a short reason.","Save work that shows the skill on new objects or data."]},"subject":"Design and Technology","success_criteria":["I can use user with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify need and explain why it matters.","I can practise problem independently on a new prompt.","I can notice decoration needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Design names the user and the problem before decoration, colour or stickers are chosen at all.","options":["Design names the user and the problem before decoration, colour or stickers are chosen at all","A claim unrelated to the observation, test or criteria","An answer copied without understanding","A guess with no evidence"],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about user?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nJordan is a Year 7 student whose pencils roll off a small homework desk and disappear underneath. A useful opening is: user Jordan; problem, pencils leave the desk during homework and are lost on the floor. One sentence that keeps both facts is: Jordan needs the pencils to stay on the desk instead of rolling away. A weak opening is a drawing of a rainbow box with no user and no problem. The rainbow does not say who is affected or what goes wrong. Decoration can wait until those two facts are written.\n\nTRY FIRST\nJordan wants rainbow stickers on the organiser before writing anything else. State the user and the problem that must be written before any colour or sticker is chosen.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe user is Jordan and the problem is pencils rolling off the desk; rainbow stickers are not the need. Jordan is a Year 7 student working at a small desk. Pencils roll over the edge and are lost underneath. A sticker colour does not keep a pencil on the desk, so it is not part of the problem statement. Write the user and the lost-pencil problem first. Choose colour only after that need is clear.","explain":"Design names the user and the problem before decoration, colour or stickers are chosen at all. A user is the person who will actually use the solution. A need is what is going wrong for that person. Jordan is a Year 7 student who does homework at a small desk. Pencils roll off the edge, drop to the floor and take time to find. The user is Jordan. The problem is lost rolling pencils, not a dull-looking desk. Stickers, a favourite colour or a fancy shape can be decided later, after the need is clear. If you decorate first, you may make something attractive that still lets pencils fall.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner base claims on observation, fair tests and design evidence, rather than guessing from a picture alone. Keep every claim linked to what was seen, measured or tested against criteria.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the observations, properties, criteria or test steps that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your record with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your evidence? If a result is uncertain, identify what would have to be observed or tested again. One deliberate, evidence-based revision is more useful than writing a longer answer. For practical technology work, follow teacher safety rules for tools, food and devices.","icon":"📖","notice":"Starting with rainbow stickers or a favourite colour before naming who loses the pencils. That mistake treats design as decorating. The pencils still roll away, so the need is untouched. Fix it by writing the user and the problem in two sentences, then leave colour until later. Correct the claim, criteria use or method, not just the neatness of the diagram.","title":"Understand and model","visual":[{"alt":"Jordan’s lost pencils contrasted with a sticker-first box that names no user.","caption":"Name the user and the rolling-pencil problem before any decoration.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">User and problem</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">Jordan loses rolling pencils</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Decoration first</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">Stickers before any need</text></g></svg>"}],"visual_before":[{"episode":{"scenes":[{"bg":"room","lines":[{"text":"I can see the example, but I am not sure which observation or criterion matters most.","who":"pip"},{"text":"Name the observations, criteria or test steps that carry the idea before you decide.","who":"juno"},{"text":"I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.","who":"pip"},{"pause_after":true,"text":"Pause here. Use this model: Jordan is a Year 7 student whose pencils roll off a small homework desk and disappear underneath. A useful opening is: u Then write your own evidence.","who":"juno"}]}],"title":"Talk through the first model","transcript":"Pip: I can see the example, but I am not sure which observation or criterion matters most.\nJuno: Name the observations, criteria or test steps that carry the idea before you decide.\nPip: I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.\nJuno: Pause here. Use this model: Jordan is a Year 7 student whose pencils roll off a small homework desk and disappear underneath. A useful opening is: u Then write your own evidence."}}]},{"check":{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Make the technology idea visible: name the observation, source or example you used and say why it supports the claim. Supported records follow the actual observations, criteria and purpose of the task. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["A claim unrelated to the observation, test or criteria","An answer copied without understanding","A guess with no evidence","Make the technology idea visible: name the observation, source or example you used and say why it supports the claim"],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about user?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nJordan is a Year 7 student whose pencils roll off a small homework desk and disappear underneath. A useful opening is: user Jordan; problem, pencils leave the desk during homework and are lost on the floor. One sentence that keeps both facts is: Jordan needs the pencils to stay on the desk instead of rolling away. A weak opening is a drawing of a rainbow box with no user and no problem. The rainbow does not say who is affected or what goes wrong. Decoration can wait until those two facts are written. Look at each observation, source detail or example that carries the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nList the key observations, sources or steps from the model and say what each one shows.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe most useful record names the evidence and the reason it supports the claim, not only a label.","explain":"Make the technology idea visible: name the observation, source or example you used and say why it supports the claim.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner base claims on observation, fair tests and design evidence, rather than guessing from a picture alone. Keep every claim linked to what was seen, measured or tested against criteria.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the observations, properties, criteria or test steps that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your record with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your evidence? If a result is uncertain, identify what would have to be observed or tested again. One deliberate, evidence-based revision is more useful than writing a longer answer. For practical technology work, follow teacher safety rules for tools, food and devices.","icon":"📖","notice":"Writing only a final label without evidence does not show technology thinking. Correct the claim, criteria use or method, not just the neatness of the diagram.","title":"Show the evidence","visual":[{"alt":"A user-and-problem sentence beside a rainbow box that does not say what goes wrong.","caption":"A design opening states who is affected and what fails.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Useful opening</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">User Jordan; pencils roll off</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Weak opening</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">Rainbow box, no problem</text></g></svg>"}]},{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Use an intentionally incorrect claim as a comparison. Name its specific technology error, then show the corrected evidence-based claim. Supported records follow the actual observations, criteria and purpose of the task. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Use an intentionally incorrect claim as a comparison","A claim unrelated to the observation, test or criteria","An answer copied without understanding","A guess with no evidence"],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about user?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nStarting with rainbow stickers or a favourite colour before naming who loses the pencils. That mistake treats design as decorating. The pencils still roll away, so the need is untouched. Fix it by writing the user and the problem in two sentences, then leave colour until later. Here is a more productive model: Jordan is a Year 7 student whose pencils roll off a small homework desk and disappear underneath. A useful opening is: user Jordan; problem, pencils leave the desk during homework and are lost on the floor. One sentence that keeps both facts is: Jordan needs the pencils to stay on the desk instead of rolling away. A weak opening is a drawing of a rainbow box with no user and no problem. The rainbow does not say who is affected or what goes wrong. Decoration can wait until those two facts are written.\n\nTRY FIRST\nDescribe why the warned-against claim fails, then write a corrected claim with evidence.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe user is Jordan and the problem is pencils rolling off the desk; rainbow stickers are not the need. Jordan is a Year 7 student working at a small desk. Pencils roll over the edge and are lost underneath. A sticker colour does not keep a pencil on the desk, so it is not part of the problem statement. Write the user and the lost-pencil problem first. Choose colour only after that need is clear.","explain":"Use an intentionally incorrect claim as a comparison. Name its specific technology error, then show the corrected evidence-based claim.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner base claims on observation, fair tests and design evidence, rather than guessing from a picture alone. Keep every claim linked to what was seen, measured or tested against criteria.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the observations, properties, criteria or test steps that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your record with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your evidence? If a result is uncertain, identify what would have to be observed or tested again. One deliberate, evidence-based revision is more useful than writing a longer answer. For practical technology work, follow teacher safety rules for tools, food and devices.","icon":"📖","notice":"Changing the wording without repairing the evidence does not fix the error. Starting with rainbow stickers or a favourite colour before naming who loses the pencils. That mistake treats design as decorating. The pencils still roll away, so the need is untouched. Fix it by writing the user and the problem in two sentences, then leave colour until later. Correct the claim, criteria use or method, not just the neatness of the diagram.","title":"Test a misconception"},{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Use the same technology idea on a new place, person, source or context, not a copy of the worked example. Review accuracy of evidence. Supported records follow the actual observations, criteria and purpose of the task. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Use the same technology idea on a new place, person, source or context, not a copy of the worked example","A claim unrelated to the observation, test or criteria","An answer copied without understanding","A guess with no evidence"],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about user?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nDesign names the user and the problem before decoration, colour or stickers are chosen at all. A user is the person who will actually use the solution. A need is what is going wrong for that person. Jordan is a Year 7 student who does homework at a small desk. Pencils roll off the edge, drop to the floor and take time to find. The user is Jordan. The problem is lost rolling pencils, not a dull-looking desk. Stickers, a favourite colour or a fancy shape can be decided later, after the need is clear. If you decorate first, you may make something attractive that still lets pencils fall. Now choose a different example to show you can transfer the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nInquire into or describe one fresh example that uses the same skill and write a short evidence-based reason.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe new example should use the same technology idea as the model, but contain different evidence.","explain":"Use the same technology idea on a new place, person, source or context, not a copy of the worked example. Review accuracy of evidence.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner base claims on observation, fair tests and design evidence, rather than guessing from a picture alone. Keep every claim linked to what was seen, measured or tested against criteria.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the observations, properties, criteria or test steps that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your record with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your evidence? If a result is uncertain, identify what would have to be observed or tested again. One deliberate, evidence-based revision is more useful than writing a longer answer. For practical technology work, follow teacher safety rules for tools, food and devices.","icon":"📖","notice":"Repeating the worked example cannot show transfer to new evidence. Correct the claim, criteria use or method, not just the neatness of the diagram.","title":"Transfer to a new investigation"}],"title":"Year 7, Lesson 1: The design process","unit_number":1,"unit_title":"The design process","week":1,"week_in_unit":1,"word_challenges":null,"worked_example":"WORK THROUGH THIS\nJordan is a Year 7 student whose pencils roll off a small homework desk and disappear underneath. A useful opening is: user Jordan; problem, pencils leave the desk during homework and are lost on the floor. One sentence that keeps both facts is: Jordan needs the pencils to stay on the desk instead of rolling away. A weak opening is a drawing of a rainbow box with no user and no problem. The rainbow does not say who is affected or what goes wrong. Decoration can wait until those two facts are written.\n\nWrite what makes this example accurate, naming the observations, criteria, properties or test steps used.","worked_visuals":[{"alt":"Jordan’s lost pencils contrasted with a sticker-first box that names no user.","caption":"Name the user and the rolling-pencil problem before any decoration.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">User and problem</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">Jordan loses rolling pencils</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Decoration first</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">Stickers before any need</text></g></svg>"},{"alt":"A user-and-problem sentence beside a rainbow box that does not say what goes wrong.","caption":"A design opening states who is affected and what fails.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Useful opening</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">User Jordan; pencils roll off</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Weak opening</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">Rainbow box, no problem</text></g></svg>"}],"year_level":"Year 7"}') WHERE seed_key='s5-design-technology-y9-w01-l1' AND family_id IS NULL;
